        "The **algorithm** is the math (the lock mechanism); the **key** is the secret that controls it (the combination). Same algorithm with a different key gives different output. Key length sets brute-force resistance (128-bit = 2^128 possibilities; 128 and 256-bit keys are infeasible to brute force today), but a long key cannot rescue a weak algorithm.",
        "**Encryption** is reversible with the key and provides confidentiality (AES, RSA). **Hashing** is one-way and fixed-length and provides integrity (SHA-256, SHA-3; MD5 and SHA-1 are broken). **Encoding** (Base64) is reversible by anyone and only changes format: Base64 data is not protected.",
