        "Host IoCs are evidence of malicious or unauthorized activity on an endpoint (workstation, server, cloud VM or container). Example: an `svchost.exe` running from Temp instead of System32, launched by a Word document and calling out to a foreign IP.",
        "Four visibility layers: processes, logs, EDR and Sysmon. Windows log channels: Security (authentication, privileges, accounts), System (services, drivers), Application, and operational channels such as PowerShell/Operational and Sysmon/Operational.",
        "Core host events: `4624`/`4625` logon success/failure, `4688` process creation (command line only if command-line auditing is enabled), `4698` scheduled task created, `4720` account created, `7045` service installed.",
        "**Sysmon** (free, Sysinternals) fills the gaps: process creation with full command line, hashes and parent (EID 1), per-process network connections (EID 3), file creation (EID 11), registry changes (EID 12–14), DNS queries (EID 22) and named pipes used by lateral movement tools.",
        "**EDR** (CrowdStrike Falcon, Defender for Endpoint, SentinelOne) adds behavioral detection, process trees and memory scanning, e.g. Word spawning PowerShell that then opens an outbound connection. Walk the whole tree; the root cause may be several parents up. A SIEM rule such as \"scheduled task created within 10 minutes of opening an email attachment\" links the steps.",
