        "**External sources**: open-source feeds (CISA KEV, AlienVault OTX, abuse.ch MalwareBazaar, Feodo Tracker and URLhaus; free and frequent, usually lower confidence), commercial intel (Recorded Future, Mandiant, CrowdStrike Falcon Intelligence; more context and confidence, at a cost), ISACs (near-real-time sector sharing) and government programs such as **CISA Automated Indicator Sharing (AIS)**. APT tracking benefits most from commercial and government intel; commodity malware is handled well by open feeds.",
        "A **threat intelligence platform (TIP)** collects, normalizes, stores, correlates and enriches, and shares threat data, then pushes it to SIEM watchlists, EDR, firewall block lists and ticketing. Its core objects are **indicators**, **threat actors**, **campaigns** (related activity over time) and **TTPs** mapped to ATT&CK, which give raw indicators operational meaning.",
