        "Dashboards give situational awareness; tuning makes what they show worth acting on. Build panels from the questions asked at every shift handover: alert volume and trend (a spike = an attack or a noisy misconfiguration), severity distribution (many mediums = tuning needed), top alert sources (one server producing 60% of alerts = a tuning problem), authentication events and network activity. Tools: Splunk Dashboard Studio, Sentinel workbooks (KQL).",
        "**Alert fatigue**: analysts facing large volumes of low-value alerts start skimming and miss real attacks. Tuning is a continuous process, not a one-time clean-up.",
        "**False positive rate** (as SOC teams usually report it): the share of a rule's alerts that are benign after triage. Track it per rule along with volume and triage time, and tune where the most analyst time is wasted.",
        "Tuning techniques: **allowlisting** (exclude the nightly vulnerability scanner from port-scan rules), **threshold adjustment** (raise 10 → 30 failed logins if IT routinely hits 15, and schedule a review), **time-based exclusions** (a 2 AM scheduled backup that looks like exfiltration), **aggregation** (one \"50 failures from one IP in 5 minutes\" alert instead of 50 alerts), plus narrower scope and context such as asset criticality to raise or lower severity.",
